add(658,'P001229','problem','Topology of Grassmannian configuration strata',
 r'''For \(E\in G(n,m)\), the notes set \(\tau_E=(P_Ee_1,\ldots,P_Ee_n)\) and ask for the topology of the stratum with prescribed combinatorial type.''',
 r'''Fix labels and a rank-\(m\) incidence or oriented-matroid type \(\mathcal M\) on \(n\) elements. Study the locus of \(m\)-planes whose coordinate projections realize \(\mathcal M\). The oriented version fixes signs of maximal minors after an orientation is chosen; the unoriented version fixes ranks of subsets. These are different stratifications.''',
 'E',r'''Configuration varieties and their normalized forms were already treated in \citet[definitions and Theorems~1--3]{mnev1985varieties}, published in 1985 from a manuscript received in July 1984. The broader question is therefore expository here, although its fixed-dimensional polytope variant below is not settled by that paper.''',[M],
 reconstruction=r'''The notebook does not choose between incidence data, an oriented matroid, and a face lattice. The statement makes these alternatives explicit rather than identifying their spaces.''',
 status=r'''As of November 2026, universality rules out a general simple classification of these strata. Exact topology requires specifying the combinatorial data and normalization.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1985-40')
add(658,'P001229','construction','Every polytope type as an orthogonal projection of a simplex',
 r'''The exercise asks to obtain combinatorially every \(m\)-dimensional convex polytope with \(n\) vertices by projecting \(S=\operatorname{conv}(e_1,\ldots,e_n)\subset\mathbb R^n\) to an \(m\)-plane.''',
 r'''If \(Q=\operatorname{conv}(v_1,\ldots,v_n)\subset\mathbb R^m\) is full-dimensional, let \(A:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m\) have columns \(v_i\), and put \(E=(\ker A)^\perp\). Then \(A|_E\) is invertible and \(A=(A|_E)P_E\). Thus \(Q\) is linearly isomorphic to \(P_E S\), so their labelled face lattices agree.''',
 'E',r'''This is an elementary linear-algebra verification of the notebook exercise; it does not require a new universality theorem. The configuration/Grassmannian framework is contemporary with \citet[p.~1312]{mnev1985varieties}.''',[M],
 reconstruction=r'''Full-dimensionality ensures that \(A\) has rank \(m\). The proof supplies the omitted linear isomorphism; the projected vertices need not equal the given numerical coordinates.''',development='annotated')
add(658,'P001229-P001230','question','Isotopy of matrices with prescribed determinant signs',
 r'''Two \(3\times n\) matrices \(x,x'\) have all minors nonzero with the same signs. Can one join them through matrices in the same class?''',
 r'''For a realizable uniform rank-three chirotope \(\chi\), is the space \(\{A\in\mathbb R^{3\times n}:\operatorname{sign}\det A_I=\chi(I)\text{ for every }I\in\binom{[n]}3\}\) path connected? With an oriented basis fixed, this is the corresponding normalized realization-space question.''',
 'E',r'''The general negative answer follows from rank-three universality already announced by \citet[Theorem~3]{mnev1985varieties}; the later complete uniform formulation is recorded in \citet[Theorem~1(iii)]{adiprasitopadrol2017universality}.''',[M,AP],
 reconstruction=r'''Maximal \(3\times3\) minors are intended in the oriented-matroid interpretation. Literally imposing signs of minors of every size is an additional condition not identified by these references.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, the general isotopy assertion is false; realization spaces may be disconnected and have much more complicated topology.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1985-41')
add(658,'P001230','claim','The small-configuration bound',
 r'''The first answer recorded is \(n\leq7\Rightarrow\) a path exists.''',
 r'''For every realizable rank-three oriented matroid on at most seven labelled elements, its normalized realization space is path connected. The uniform version gives the positive answer to the preceding question.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[introduction, p.~287]{tsukamoto2013disconnected} records the stronger established bound of fewer than nine elements, citing the classical small-configuration literature. This verifies the notebook inequality but does not identify its precise historical source.''',[TS],
 status=r'''As of November 2026, the stated bound is valid and not optimal as a general small-size assertion.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',problem='GP-1985-41')
add(658,'P001230','claim','The nineteen-column counterexample attributed to Mnëv',
 r'''The notes state \(n=19\Rightarrow\) no path, with N.~E.~Mn\"ev named.''',
 r'''There should exist two realizations of a single uniform rank-three oriented matroid on nineteen labelled elements lying in distinct path components of its realization space.''',
 'F',r'''The qualitative counterexample is compatible with Mn\"ev universality, but the exact nineteen-element construction and its uniformity were not located in the opened primary texts. \citet[Theorem~1.3]{tsukamoto2013disconnected} constructs thirteen-element disconnected rank-three spaces with prescribed collinearities; these are not substitutes for the notebook\textquotesingle s nonzero-minor assertion.''',[M,TS],
 reconstruction=r'''The formulation assumes that ``all minors'' means maximal minors. A later smaller nonuniform example does not verify this exact claim.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, the general phenomenon is established; the notebook\textquotesingle s exact numerical example remains an unresolved identification in this companion.''',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='unresolved lead',problem='GP-1985-41',confidence='low')
add(658,'P001230','theorem','Universality for real configuration spaces',
 r'''The topology can be that of any real algebraic variety.''',
 r'''Every primary basic semialgebraic set defined over \(\mathbb Z\) is stably equivalent to a realization space of a rank-three oriented matroid. For an open such set, the oriented matroid may be chosen uniform. In particular these spaces realize arbitrary finite simplicial-complex homotopy types; equality or homeomorphism to an arbitrary variety is not asserted.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Theorem~3]{mnev1985varieties} announced the open-set version before the seminar. The standard stable-equivalence formulation and the open/uniform qualification are stated in \citet[Theorem~1]{adiprasitopadrol2017universality}; the latter first circulated in February 2014, appeared online in May 2016, and was printed in 2017.''',[M,AP],
 reconstruction=r'''Stable equivalence, integral defining equations and the uniform/open restriction replace the notebook\textquotesingle s informal universal quantifier. These qualifications carry arithmetic information beyond homotopy type.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1985-40')
add(658,'P001230','question','A complex analogue of convexity',
 r'''Over \(\mathbb C\), the analogue is said to be computing \(\pi_1\); the notes then ask what the convex analogue over \(\mathbb C\) is.''',
 r'''Two prompts are recorded: determine fundamental groups of suitably defined complex configuration strata, and identify an appropriate complex counterpart of real convex-polytopal realization spaces. No category of complex convex objects or equivalence relation is specified.''',
 'F',r'''The real universality statements in \citet{mnev1985varieties} do not select a unique complex problem. Searches for complex configuration varieties and realization spaces did not identify an exact result matching both prompts.''',[M],status=r'''As of November 2026, no precise open problem is extracted: choosing a complex analogue would itself be an editorial addition.''',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
add(658,'P001230','theorem','Steinitz characterization of three-dimensional polytope graphs',
 r'''Steinitz\textquotesingle s theorem is cited as characterizing the one-dimensional skeleton of a convex polytope in \(\mathbb R^3\).''',
 r'''A finite simple graph is the graph of a convex three-dimensional polytope if and only if it is planar and three-vertex-connected.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[section~2]{richtergebertziegler1995realization} restates the classical theorem and dates the original result to Steinitz, 1922. The theorem predates this seminar by decades.''',[RG])
add(658,'P001230','theorem','Contractibility of normalized three-polytope realization spaces',
 r'''A ``corollary'' says that the space of three-polytopes of a fixed combinatorial type is contractible.''',
 r'''For a realizable labelled three-polytope type, its realization space after fixing an affine basis is contractible; a standard normalized realization-space model is an open ball. The unnormalized collection of all vertex coordinates is not generally contractible.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[section~2, Steinitz theorem]{richtergebertziegler1995realization} gives the normalized open-ball assertion. \citet[p.~1312]{mnev1985varieties} explicitly describes orientation components in an unnormalized model, showing why the normalization cannot be omitted.''',[RG,M],
 reconstruction=r'''An affine basis and orientation component must be specified. This does not follow merely from the graph characterization without the stronger realization-space form of Steinitz\textquotesingle s theorem.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(658,'P001230','claim','Universality for simplicial four-polytope realization spaces',
 r'''In \(\mathbb R^4\), even simplicial polytopes are said to have \ru{более или менее любой} (``more or less any'') topology for a fixed combinatorial type.''',
 r'''A precise homotopy interpretation would ask: for every nonempty finite simplicial complex \(K\), is there a labelled simplicial four-polytope whose appropriately normalized realization space is homotopy equivalent to \(K\)? The notebook neither specifies this equivalence nor supplies exact quantifiers. A stronger literature conjecture asks for stable equivalence to every nonempty open primary basic semialgebraic set, and even requires the four-polytope to be neighborly.''',
 'C',r'''General four-polytope universality was proved in work of 1994 and published by \citet[Main Theorem]{richtergebertziegler1995realization} in 1995. \citet[Theorem~2]{adiprasitopadrol2017universality}, circulated in 2014, published online in 2016 and printed in 2017, proves simplicial/neighborly universality with dimension growing as \(2n-4\); their Conjecture~4 retains dimension four and additionally requires neighborliness. These are distinct results. Adiprasito--Padrol explicitly judge the earlier simplicial proof incomplete and supply its missing argument; the 1985 announcement is not itself a fully checked proof of that part.''',[RG,AP,'schaefercardinalmiltzow2024existential'],
 reconstruction=r'''The displayed homotopy question is an editorial sharpening of the hedge, not a verbatim conjecture attributed to Vershik. Stable-equivalence and homotopy versions must remain separate. Adiprasito--Padrol use a homogenized cone-realization model modulo \(\mathrm{GL}_{d+1}(\mathbb R)\), whereas the bounded proof here fixes an affine frame and a stacking history; their normalizations are not silently identified.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, no resolution of fixed-dimensional simplicial universality was found in this bounded search. The latest opened source explicitly recording the constant-dimension simplicial problem as open is the 2024 compendium, entries CG68--CG69. The chapter proves only a bounded contractibility result for fixed sequences of facet stackings.''',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='read the result',development='developed in this chapter''',problem='GP-1985-42''',worth=r'''Worth developing in bounded families: explicit insertion fibers distinguish tractable history-compatible spaces from the unresolved universal quantifier.''',confidence='high')
add(658,'P001230','problem','Fields of polytopes in tangent spaces',
 r'''The stated task is to construct a field of polytopes, one in every tangent space of a manifold; a classification problem is mentioned.''',
 r'''For a smooth manifold \(M\), one could prescribe a convex-polytope type \(P\) and ask for a continuous section of the bundle of copies of \(P\) inside \(TM\), then classify such sections up to a specified equivalence. The notebook does not prescribe regularity, a type, or equivalence.''',
 'F',r'''\citet[bibliography, item~6]{mnev1985varieties} points to Vershik--Chernyakov\textquotesingle s 1982 work on fields of cones as a nearby motivation. This is a lead, not a verified solution of the notebook\textquotesingle s classification programme.''',[M],reconstruction=r'''The associated-bundle formulation is a possible modern interpretation only.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, the recorded programme is too broad to assign a definite unresolved theorem or a new problem-index identifier.''',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='low')
add(658,'P001230','definition','Geometric lattices and finite matroids',
 r'''A geometric lattice is described as semimodular and ``finite'', with a parenthesis saying that every element is a finite join of atoms.''',
 r'''A finite lattice is geometric when it is atomistic and upper semimodular. Atomistic means every element is a join of atoms; finiteness and atomisticity are separate requirements. The lattice of flats of a finite simple matroid is geometric, and conversely every finite geometric lattice arises this way.''',
 'E',r'''This is classical matroid theory, not a new definition in 1985. \citet[sections~1--2]{oxleybriefly} provides a modern author exposition of the underlying finite-matroid definitions; the notebook\textquotesingle s conflation is corrected explicitly.''',[OX],literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='indirect')
add(658,'P001230','question','Representability of a geometric lattice by a vector configuration',
 r'''After ``configuration --- geometric lattice'', the converse question asks when a geometric lattice is given by a configuration.''',
 r'''Given a finite geometric lattice \(L\) and a specified field \(F\), does there exist a finite vector configuration over \(F\) whose lattice of flats is isomorphic to \(L\)? For \(F=\mathbb R\), prescribed ranks can be expressed by vanishing minors and nonvanishing minors, producing a finite semialgebraic feasibility problem.''',
 'E',r'''The field dependence is fundamental in classical representability; see \citet[section~4, Theorem~4.1]{oxleybriefly}. The semialgebraic encoding places real representability in the setting of \citet{mnev1985varieties}; it is not a simple combinatorial criterion.''',[OX,M],reconstruction=r'''The field is supplied explicitly. This matroid representation problem is not equivalent to arbitrary join-preserving embeddings of the same lattice into a subspace lattice.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, individual finite real instances are decidable by real quantifier elimination, while universality explains their potential complexity.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
add(658,'P001230','construction','The Fano configuration and characteristic two',
 r'''The seven-point diagram is called the Fano configuration and declared unrealizable over \(\mathbb R\) or \(\mathbb Z\). The scan shows only six of the seven Fano lines.''',
 r'''The Fano matroid \(F_7\) is representable over a field \(F\) exactly when \(\operatorname{char}F=2\). A representation in characteristic two uses the seven nonzero columns of \(\mathbb F_2^3\). No real matrix has exactly its rank incidences; consequently no integer matrix does either.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Theorem~4.1]{oxleybriefly} states the exact characteristic criterion. The result is classical and predates the talk.''',[OX],reconstruction=r'''The named Fano matroid supplies the missing seventh line, but no absent stroke is restored to the manuscript diagram. The characteristic claim is valid for the named object, not for every seven-point drawing with those six lines.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(658,'P001230-P001231','definition','Faithful join representations and an unresolved subspace example',
 r'''A faithful representation \(a:\Gamma\to\mathcal L(F^n)\) is required to preserve strict order and suprema. The following question-marked fragment mentions \(m=4\), \(E,F\), and \(\mathbb R^6\).''',
 r'''An injective join-semilattice map satisfies \(a(x\vee y)=a(x)+a(y)\) and preserves strict order. It need not preserve meets unless that is imposed separately. The fragment involving four-dimensional subspaces of \(\mathbb R^6\) does not determine a particular lattice or a theorem.''',
 'F',r'''\citet[section~1]{gelfandponomarev1974modular} is a genuine pre-1985 source on representations of modular lattices, but the stated join-only definition and the incomplete dimensional fragment do not identify one of its theorems.''', ['gelfandponomarev1974modular'''],status=r'As of November 2026, the definition is interpretable; the intended four-dimensional example and claimed representability remain unidentified.''',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='low')
add(658,'P001231','claim','Representability and systems of algebraic inequalities',
 r'''The notes identify the realization task with solving a system of algebraic inequalities.''',
 r'''For a fixed finite real vector-configuration type, rank constraints can be written as polynomial equalities and strict nonvanishing conditions on minors. Conversely, real configuration realization problems are universal for suitable primary basic semialgebraic sets up to stable equivalence. An arbitrary faithful join embedding of a lattice is a different representation notion and is not covered without further specifications.''',
 'E',r'''The universality direction is already present in \citet[Theorem~3]{mnev1985varieties}; \citet[Theorem~1]{adiprasitopadrol2017universality} gives the modern precise formulation.''',[M,AP],reconstruction=r'''The forward encoding and the converse universality statement are separated. Strict inequalities, equations and the equivalence relation cannot all be suppressed.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1985-40')
add(658,'P001231','remark','Gelfand on lattices and their representation theory',
 r'''Gelfand names Dedekind, Kurosh, Ore, von Neumann, and work with Ponomarev in discussing structures/lattices.''',
 r'''The intervention situates the representation of lattices by subspaces within an older algebraic programme. It contains no further explicitly stated classification theorem.''',
 'E',r'''The concrete Gelfand--Ponomarev connection is \citet{gelfandponomarev1974modular}, published in 1974. The list of names alone does not warrant importing additional results.''', ['gelfandponomarev1974modular'''],status=r'As of November 2026, this is a historical attribution, not an independent open problem.''',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
add(658,'P001231','theorem','Every finite lattice embeds in a finite partition lattice',
 r'''A theorem attributed to Pudl\'ak and T\r{u}ma embeds every finite lattice in a lattice of equivalence relations; infimum is intersection and supremum is described using union.''',
 r'''For every finite lattice \(L\), there exists a finite set \(X\) and an injective lattice homomorphism \(L\hookrightarrow\operatorname{Eq}(X)\). Meets in \(\operatorname{Eq}(X)\) are intersections; joins are the equivalence relations generated by unions, not the raw unions.''',
 'E',r'''The preliminary announcement is \citet{pudlaktuma1977every}. \citet[Conclusions]{pudlaktuma1980finite} gives a bounded embedding into \(\operatorname{Eq}(n)\) for sufficiently large \(n\); the full article appeared in December 1980 after receipt in 1977.''',[PT,'pudlaktuma1977every'],reconstruction=r'''The lattice operations are made explicit. This embedding theorem is not the finite algebra congruence-lattice representation problem.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
