add(659,'P001231','definition','Elastic scattering and Mandelstam invariants',
 r'''For equal masses, \(p_1+q_1=p_2+q_2\), all four squares equal \(m^2\), and \(s=(p_1+q_1)^2\), \(t=(p_1-p_2)^2\), \(u=(p_1-q_2)^2\), with \(s+t+u=4m^2\). The notes also say total momentum is zero.''',
 r'''For four on-shell Minkowski momenta with the stated conservation law, the displayed invariants obey \(s+t+u=4m^2\) by bilinear expansion. A zero total spatial momentum may specify the centre-of-mass frame; a zero sum of all incoming four-momenta requires reversing the outgoing momenta.''',
 'E',r'''This is standard kinematic input to the pre-1985 dual-resonance construction, as in \citet{veneziano1968construction}. The invariant identity is verified directly from the notebook equations.''',[V],reconstruction=r'''The frame convention is supplied to avoid interpreting positive-energy incoming four-momenta as having zero total energy.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='publisher metadata checked',development='annotated')
add(659,'P001231-P001232','definition','The listed duality conditions',
 r'''The amplitude is analytic, symmetric in \(s,t\), has only simple \(s\)-poles at positions independent of \(t\), and has polynomial residues \(R_n(t)\) in \(\sum_{n\geq0}R_n(t)/(n-\alpha(s))\).''',
 r'''The intended class consists of meromorphic crossing-symmetric functions whose pole divisors are \(\alpha(s)=n\) and \(\alpha(t)=n\), with polynomial residues in the crossed variable when \(\alpha\) is affine. A partial-fraction series additionally needs a convergence prescription or subtraction terms.''',
 'E',r'''The conditions belong to the historical dual-amplitude programme of \citet{veneziano1968construction}. Only publisher metadata was accessible for that original paper; the notebook conditions themselves are analyzed here without attributing a completeness theorem to it.''',[V],reconstruction=r'''``Analytic'' means holomorphic off the poles, not entire. Growth and normalization conditions are absent.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='publisher metadata checked')
add(659,'P001232','claim','The Veneziano beta amplitude and the unsupported uniqueness assertion',
 r'''The five conditions are said to determine uniquely \(A(s,t)=B(-\alpha(s),-\alpha(t))\).''',
 r'''The beta function gives one meromorphic crossing-symmetric solution. For affine \(\alpha\), its residue at \(\alpha(s)=n\) is polynomial in \(\alpha(t)\). The listed properties alone do not fix even its overall nonzero scalar factor; unless a fully convergent pole expansion without an entire part is imposed, adding a crossing-symmetric polynomial also preserves the listed local pole properties.''',
 'E',r'''The amplitude was published by \citet{veneziano1968construction} in September 1968, after receipt on 29 July. No uniqueness theorem under exactly the notebook hypotheses was verified; elementary counterexamples show why extra assumptions are necessary.''',[V],status=r'''As of November 2026, the beta-function construction is classical; uniqueness under only the recorded conditions is false.''',literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',development='annotated')
add(659,'P001232','remark','Linear Regge trajectories as physical input',
 r'''The linearity of \(\alpha(s)\) is attributed to analysis of experimental data.''',
 r'''The model takes \(\alpha(s)=\alpha_0+\alpha's\), with intercept and slope supplied as parameters. This is a phenomenological assumption in the historical amplitude, not a mathematical consequence of the five conditions.''',
 'E',r'''The original title of \citet{veneziano1968construction} explicitly specifies linearly rising trajectories. The notebook supplies no experiment or numerical fit to audit.''',[V],status=r'''As of November 2026, this item is a historical model assumption; no claim of exact linearity for all observed hadrons is inferred.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked')
add(659,'P001232','construction','Euler integral for the four-point amplitude',
 r'''The beta amplitude is rewritten as a one-variable integral with exponents involving pairwise momentum products; the accompanying two-in/two-out diagram is followed by \(\sum_i p_i=0\).''',
 r'''For \(\operatorname{Re}\alpha(s)<0\) and \(\operatorname{Re}\alpha(t)<0\), \[B(-\alpha(s),-\alpha(t))=\int_0^1 x^{-\alpha(s)-1}(1-x)^{-\alpha(t)-1}\,dx.\] Other values are defined by meromorphic continuation. Expressing the exponents through momenta additionally fixes the metric, mass shell, slope and all-incoming convention.''',
 'E',r'''This is Euler\textquotesingle s beta integral in the amplitude of \citet{veneziano1968construction}; no post-1985 mathematical development is needed.''',[V],reconstruction=r'''The unambiguous beta-integral formula replaces an identification of physical parameters that the notes do not fully normalize. Outgoing arrows alone do not implement all-incoming signed momenta.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='publisher metadata checked')
add(659,'P001232','claim','Five-point Euler integrals and the meaning of closed evaluation',
 r'''A double integral with factors \(x,y,1-x,1-y,1-xy\) and omitted exponents is said not to have been calculated in general.''',
 r'''For parameters \(a,b,c,d>0\) and real \(e<b+d\), the model integral \[\int_0^1\!\int_0^1x^{a-1}(1-x)^{b-1}y^{c-1}(1-y)^{d-1}(1-xy)^{-e}\,dx\,dy\] equals \[B(a,b)B(c,d)\,{}_3F_2\!\left(\begin{matrix}a,c,e\\a+b,c+d\end{matrix};1\right).\] This follows by the binomial expansion and beta integration, with continuation where justified. A special-function representation is different from a gamma-product evaluation.''',
 'F',r'''The notebook omits the exponents and the intended class of answers. The displayed elementary reconstruction establishes that ``not calculated'' cannot be promoted to a precise general open problem. The four-point comparator is \citet{veneziano1968construction}.''',[V],reconstruction=r'''The five-parameter integral and its evaluation are an editorial calculation, not a formula supplied by the notebook. No exact physical five-point amplitude or gamma-product conjecture is claimed.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, the notebook\textquotesingle s intended evaluation question is not identifiable.''',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',development='annotated',confidence='medium')
add(659,'P001232-P001233','construction','The Heisenberg algebra and infinitely many string modes',
 r'''Generators \(a_m^\mu,x^\mu\) satisfy \([a_m^\mu,a_n^\nu]=mg^{\mu\nu}\delta_{m,-n}\), \(a_0=p\), \([x^\mu,p^\nu]=ig^{\mu\nu}\), and \([x,a_m]=0\) for \(m\ne0\). The text first says finitely many oscillators and immediately says finitely many do not suffice.''',
 r'''Let \(g\) be a nondegenerate form on a \(d\)-dimensional space, indexed by \(0\leq\mu<d\). Use one oscillator of each positive integer frequency in each component, together with zero modes. The algebraic Fock space consists of finite products of negative-frequency generators acting on a momentum vacuum; infinitely many generators exist although each vector uses finitely many.''',
 'E',r'''The same Heisenberg representation is written in \citet[introduction, equations~(1)--(2)]{frenkel1985representations}, tracing the construction to the early dual-resonance literature.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The index endpoint is corrected from \(d\) to \(d-1\). Finitely many species and countably many frequencies resolve the contradictory prose.''',literal='false as written',match='partial')
add(659,'P001233','construction','Chiral coordinate fields and their distributional commutators',
 r'''The fields are \(P(\theta)=\sum_m a_me^{-im\theta}\) and \(Q(\theta)=x+p\theta+i\sum_{n\ne0}a_n e^{-in\theta}/n\). Their commutators involve \(\delta\), \(\delta'\), and a function called Heaviside.''',
 r'''With the corrected common index \(n\) in \(Q\), \(\partial_\theta Q=P\). In the distributional sense, \([Q^\mu(\theta),P^\nu(\theta')]=2\pi i g^{\mu\nu}\delta_{2\pi}(\theta-\theta')\) and \([P^\mu(\theta),P^\nu(\theta')]=2\pi i g^{\mu\nu}\delta'_{2\pi}(\theta-\theta')\). On a lifted interval with \(0<|\theta-\theta'|<2\pi\), \([Q^\mu(\theta),Q^\nu(\theta')]=-i\pi g^{\mu\nu}\operatorname{sgn}(\theta-\theta')\), extended by the zero-mode monodromy.''',
 'E',r'''These identities follow directly from the displayed oscillator brackets; the field formalism is the one used in \citet[introduction, equations~(1)--(4)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The plain Heaviside function cannot be the antisymmetric commutator kernel. The local sign function, a lift of the angular coordinate, and distributional interpretation are supplied.''',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
add(659,'P001233-P001234','theorem','Sugawara operators for free bosons',
 r'''\(L_m=(4\pi)^{-1}\int e^{im\theta}:P(\theta)^2:\,d\theta\), equivalently \(2L_m=\sum_{r+s=m}:a_ra_s:\), is asserted to satisfy Virasoro with central charge \(d\); this is an exercise. The formula for \(L_0\) is given.''',
 r'''On algebraic Fock space, \[L_m=\tfrac12\sum_{r\in\mathbb Z}g_{\mu\nu}:a_r^\mu a_{m-r}^\nu:,\qquad L_0=\tfrac12(p,p)+\sum_{n>0}(a_{-n},a_n),\] and \[[L_m,L_n]=(m-n)L_{m+n}+\tfrac d{12}(m^3-m)\delta_{m,-n}I.\] Normal ordering puts annihilation operators on the right; each mode acts by a finite sum on a fixed finite-energy vector.''',
 'E',r'''The construction and the double-contraction calculation are given in \citet[section~2 and Theorem~3.3]{frenkel1985representations}. The signature of \(g\) does not change the central charge \(d\).''',[F],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(659,'P001234','construction','Normal-ordered exponential vertex operators',
 r'''\(V(k,\theta)=\exp(i\langle k,Q(\theta)\rangle)\) is factorized at \(\theta=0\) into creation and annihilation exponentials with a momentum-shift factor \(e^{ikx}\).''',
 r'''The exponential must be normal ordered. At \(z=1\), one may write \[Y_k(1)=\exp\!\left(\sum_{n>0}(k,a_{-n})/n\right)D_k\exp\!\left(-\sum_{n>0}(k,a_n)/n\right).\] The complete field is \(Y_k(z)=E_-(k,z)D_k z^{(k,a_0)}E_+(k,z)\), with an additional lattice cocycle when mutual locality is required.''',
 'E',r'''Compare \citet[section~2.1, equation~(2.3)]{frenkel1985representations} and the original affine construction of \citet{frenkelkac1980basic} (published in 1980).''',[F,FK],reconstruction=r'''Normal ordering and the zero-mode power for variable \(z\) are supplied; an unregularized exponential of the field has divergent self-contraction.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(659,'P001234','theorem','Vertex covariance and conformal weight',
 r'''The notes give \(V(k,\theta)=e^{i\theta L_0}V(k)e^{-i\theta L_0}\) and \([L_n-L_0,V(k)]=n(k,k)V(k)/2\).''',
 r'''For the plane primary field \(Y_k(z)\) of weight \(h=(k,k)/2\), \[[L_n,Y_k(z)]=\bigl(z^{n+1}\partial_z+(n+1)hz^n\bigr)Y_k(z).\] Evaluation at \(z=1\) gives the stated difference commutator. Angular translation and the cylinder field require a consistent choice of \(z=e^{\pm i\theta}\) and the factor \(z^h\).''',
 'E',r'''This is the standard free-boson covariance relation; \citet[section~2, vertex and Virasoro relations]{frenkel1985representations} provides the pre-1985 operator construction.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The field of conformal weight \(h\), its plane/cylinder convention, and the zero-mode factor are essential. The note\textquotesingle s two angular signs should not be combined without this convention choice.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
add(659,'P001232,P001234','method','Factorizing amplitudes through vertices and a propagator',
 r'''The proposed vacuum expectation becomes a chain \(\langle0|V(p_2)D(s_2)V(p_3)\cdots D(s_{n-2})V(p_{n-1})|0\rangle\), with \(D(s)=\int_0^1x^{L_0-\alpha(s)-1}(1-x)^{\alpha(0)-1}\,dx\).''',
 r'''On an eigenvector of the operator denoted by \(L_0\) with eigenvalue \(\lambda\), the displayed propagator is \(B(\lambda-\alpha(s),\alpha(0))\), initially for positive real parts of both arguments, and then meromorphically continued. In a full string amplitude one must specify whether this operator is the oscillator number operator or the full zero-mode-inclusive \(L_0\), the incoming/outgoing vacuum momenta, and vertex normalizations.''',
 'E',r'''The method belongs to the established dual-resonance operator formalism discussed by \citet[introduction]{frenkel1985representations}. No exact equality of the notebook\textquotesingle s incompletely normalized chain with every physical amplitude is asserted.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The spectral meaning of the integral is made explicit. The notebook reuses \(L_0\) in a way that may double-count the intermediate momentum if read literally.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated')
add(659,'P001234-P001235','construction','Momentum Fock spaces and mode shifts',
 r'''Vacua satisfy \(a_n|0,p\rangle=0\) for \(n>0\) and \(a_0|0,p\rangle=p|0,p\rangle\). Vertex modes map \(H(p)\to H(p+k)\) and satisfy \([a_m^\mu,V_n(k)]=k_\mu V_{n+m}(k)\).''',
 r'''Let \(H(p)=S(\bigoplus_{n>0}\mathbb C^d t^{-n})|p\rangle\). Then \([a_0^\mu,Y_k(z)]=k^\mu Y_k(z)\) and \([a_m^\mu,Y_k(z)]=k^\mu z^mY_k(z)\). With the convention \(Y_k(z)=\sum_nY_{k,n}z^{-n}\), the mode relation is \([a_m^\mu,Y_{k,n}]=k^\mu Y_{k,n+m}\). Integral mode labels require the appropriate integral momentum pairings; otherwise shifted powers occur.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[section~2.1, equations~(2.2)--(2.4)]{frenkel1985representations} gives this construction. The upper/lower target index must be raised or lowered consistently with \(g\).''',[F],reconstruction=r'''An algebraic sum over chosen momenta is sufficient for formal fields; a Hilbert-space direct integral over continuous momenta is a different completion.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(659,'P001235','theorem','The two-vertex contraction and binomial generating series',
 r'''An exercise asserts \(\sum_n A_nz^n=(1-z)^{(k_1,k_2)}\) for two vertex modes. The final momentum in the bra is written \(p+k_1+k\), without the second subscript.''',
 r'''For the oscillator parts of two normal-ordered vertices, in the radial region \(|z|<1\), contraction gives \[\exp\!\left(-(k_1,k_2)\sum_{n>0}\frac{z^n}{n}\right)=(1-z)^{(k_1,k_2)}.\] Thus the normalized oscillator coefficients are \(A_n=(-1)^n\binom{(k_1,k_2)}n\). The full matrix element also contains the zero-mode monomial and has final momentum \(p+k_1+k_2\).''',
 'E',r'''The calculation is the two-field instance of the vertex product formula in \citet[equation~(2.5)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''``Normalized oscillator coefficients'' separates the stated contraction from omitted zero-mode powers and the possible shift of Laurent indices.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001236','definition','Virasoro weights and the physical constraint space',
 r'''The continuation introduces weight vectors, highest weight, and \(\widetilde{\mathcal H}=\{v:L_0v=v,\ L_iv=0\ (i>0)\}\).''',
 r'''For a Virasoro module with diagonalizable \(L_0\), the relation \([L_0,L_n]=-nL_n\) makes \(L_n\) lower the \(L_0\)-eigenvalue by \(n\). The physical constraint space at intercept one is \(P^1=\ker(L_0-1)\cap\bigcap_{n>0}\ker L_n\). It is a subspace of highest-weight vectors, not a Virasoro submodule in general.''',
 'E',r'''This is the standard constraint space in \citet[equations~(8) and (4.1)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(660,'P001236','definition','Spurious states and the physical quotient',
 r'''The set of spurions is written as those physical \(v\) expressible as one \(L_iv'\), \(i<0\), and the physical space is its quotient.''',
 r'''In an algebraic free-boson Fock module with its invariant Hermitian form, put \(S=\sum_{n>0}L_{-n}H\) and \(N=P^1\cap S\). The linear quotient \(P^1/N\) is well defined. In each finite-dimensional energy space, adjointness \(L_n^*=L_{-n}\) identifies \(N\) with the radical of the restricted form on \(P^1\). A union of individual images need not be a subspace.''',
 'E',r'''The transverse/spurious decomposition occurs in \citet[section~4, Lemmas~4.2--4.3]{frenkel1985representations}; \citet[section~5, Theorem~5.1]{borcherds1992monstrous} explicitly uses the quotient by the null space in a later application.''',[F,B],reconstruction=r'''The sum of images and the invariant form are supplied. The equivalence with the radical uses finite-dimensionality of each fixed-momentum energy level and its nondegenerate ambient form.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001236','claim','The weight of a preimage under L minus one',
 r'''Equation (5) says that if \(L_{-1}v'=v\) has weight one then \(v'\) has weight two.''',
 r'''If \(v'\) is a weight vector, \(L_{-1}v'\ne0\), and \(L_0L_{-1}v'=L_{-1}v'\), then \(L_0v'=0\). Indeed, \([L_0,L_{-1}]=L_{-1}\), so \(L_{-1}\) raises the weight by one.''',
 'E',r'''The correction is forced by the Virasoro bracket, for example \citet[equation~(2.9)]{frenkel1985representations}. The scan clearly contains both the word ``two'' and \(2v'\); this is a faithful mathematical error, not a translation correction.''',[F],literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001236','method','Half constraints followed by a quotient',
 r'''Genuine invariants under diffeomorphisms are obstructed by the nontrivial centre. The proposed procedure takes invariants for one half and quotients by the action of the other half.''',
 r'''The construction \(P^1/(P^1\cap\sum_{n>0}L_{-n}H)\) imposes the positive-mode constraints and removes the spurious descendants. It is not the subspace annihilated by every Virasoro operator: with nonzero central charge, such an ordinary invariant subspace is zero.''',
 'E',r'''The physical quotient is the established pre-1985 construction summarized in \citet[section~4]{frenkel1985representations}. A full cohomological or BRST reformulation is not specified in this notebook passage.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The quotation marks around ``invariants'' are retained in substance; no unstated equivalence with an arbitrary cohomology theory is attributed to the speaker.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
add(660,'P001236','theorem','The critical no-ghost theorem',
 r'''The two goals are a description by \(d-2\) oscillators and strict positivity on the physical quotient; both are said to hold for \(d=26\). The parenthesis calls this absence of tachyons.''',
 r'''For the bosonic Fock space with one negative and twenty-five positive directions, intercept one, and nonzero real momentum \(p\), the form on \(P^1(p)\) is positive semidefinite. Its quotient by the radical is positive definite and has the transverse oscillator multiplicities of twenty-four bosons at level \(N=1-(p,p)/2\), when \(N\) is a nonnegative integer. Strict positivity applies to nonzero quotient vectors. Zero momentum must be excluded: its level-one physical space contains the full indefinite target space.''',
 'E',r'''No-ghost was established in the early 1970s. The exact zero-momentum exception and the critical transverse proof can be checked in \citet[Theorem~4.1 and Lemmas~4.2--4.3]{frenkel1985representations}; a later general form is \citet[Theorem~5.1]{borcherds1992monstrous}.''',[F,B],reconstruction=r'''The momentum hypothesis, quotient by null vectors and level condition are supplied. Positivity removes negative-norm ghosts; it does not remove the bosonic ground-state tachyon.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, the theorem is classical. The notebook assertion becomes correct with these hypotheses; its identification with absence of tachyons is false.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001237','construction','The creation coefficient and zero modes in the continued vertex formula',
 r'''Equation (6) writes the creation exponential without \(1/n\), followed by \(D_p\) and the correctly divided annihilation exponential. Equation (7) defines the momentum shift.''',
 r'''The oscillator factors must both have frequency denominators: \(E_-(p,z)=\exp(\sum_{n>0}(p,a_{-n})z^n/n)\) and \(E_+(p,z)=\exp(-\sum_{n>0}(p,a_n)z^{-n}/n)\). The plane field is \(Y_p(z)=E_-(p,z)D_pz^{(p,a_0)}E_+(p,z)\). Its cylinder-normalized counterpart is \(X_p(z)=z^{(p,p)/2}Y_p(z)\), up to consistently ordered zero-mode factors.''',
 'E',r'''Compare \citet[equations~(2.2)--(2.3)]{frenkel1985representations}. In that source the exponential of the sum of zero-mode operators incorporates a scalar power through their commutator; replacing it by a bare translation loses this factor.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The scan confirms the missing creation denominator. Plane and cylinder fields are distinguished so that the subsequent \(dz/z\) residue is meaningful.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001237','theorem','Multipoint oscillator contractions',
 r'''Equation (8) gives a vacuum matrix element as \(\prod_{j>1}(1-z_j/z_i)^{(p_i,p_j)}\).''',
 r'''For corrected, oscillator-stripped vertices in the radial region \(|z_1|>\cdots>|z_r|\), their normalized contraction is \[\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq r}(1-z_j/z_i)^{(p_i,p_j)}.\] A full matrix element of \(Y_{p_i}\), between initial momentum \(k\) and final momentum \(k+\sum_i p_i\), also contains the zero-mode monomial \(\prod_i z_i^{(p_i,k+\sum_{j>i}p_j)}\), as well as any chosen cocycle factors.''',
 'E',r'''The repeated two-field contraction follows from \citet[equation~(2.5)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The product index is changed from \(j>1\) to \(i<j\); the extra powers assume the displayed order of plane-field zero modes. The oscillator identity is not the full correlation function without this qualification.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001237','theorem','Norm-two screening operators commute with Virasoro',
 r'''The integrals \(\int X(p,z)\,dz/z\) are to commute with Virasoro when \((p,p)=2\).''',
 r'''If \(Y_p(z)\) is a primary field of weight one with integral Laurent powers on the chosen momentum sectors, then \(Q_p=\operatorname{Res}_{z=0}Y_p(z)\,dz\) commutes with every \(L_n\), because its commutator is the residue of \(\partial_z(z^{n+1}Y_p)\). For \(X_p=zY_p\), this is equivalently \(\operatorname{Res}X_p(z)\,dz/z\). Free-boson weight one means \((p,p)=2\).''',
 'E',r'''The commuting zero modes are part of \citet[equations~(2.10)--(2.12)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The notebook operator lacks the necessary zero-mode normalization; its formula cannot be used literally in this residue assertion. Residue notation replaces an unspecified contour and factor \(2\pi i\).''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001237','construction','A symmetric Cartan matrix from Lorentzian vectors',
 r'''Vectors \(p_i\in\mathbb R^{26}\) have square two and integral products \(A_{ij}\), followed by the unrestricted inequality \(A_{ij}<0\).''',
 r'''The intended symmetric generalized Cartan conditions are \(A_{ii}=2\), \(A_{ij}\in\mathbb Z_{\leq0}\) for \(i\ne j\), and \(A_{ij}=0\iff A_{ji}=0\). A realization as a Gram matrix in signature \((25,1)\) is an additional restriction: its positive and negative inertia indices are at most twenty-five and one, respectively, and its rank is at most twenty-six.''',
 'E',r'''The exact Cartan conditions appear in \citet[section~1.1]{frenkel1985representations}. No assertion that every symmetric Cartan matrix fits this fixed Lorentzian space is warranted.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The negative sign applies off the diagonal and allows zero. The inertia restrictions are elementary consequences of a Gram realization.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(660,'P001237','theorem','Kac--Moody Chevalley operators from screening residues',
 r'''The norm-two vertex residues \(e_i,f_i\), together with \(h_i|k\rangle=(k,p_i)|k\rangle\), are asserted to represent the algebra with Cartan matrix \(A\). Macdonald at Bourbaki is recommended.''',
 r'''For a symmetric nonaffine Kac--Moody algebra and its standard lattice/Heisenberg vertex construction, a compatible bimultiplicative sign cocycle gives screening operators for the simple roots and their negatives satisfying the Chevalley--Serre relations. The sign of \(f_i\) is chosen to match either \([e_i,f_i]=h_i\) or the opposite Chevalley convention. In the affine case one uses the separate basic current-algebra construction, rather than assuming the same nondegenerate root-space model.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Theorem~2.1]{frenkel1985representations} proves the nonaffine statement and explicitly separates its affine Theorem~2.2. The suggested reading is identifiable as \citet{macdonald1981affine}, Bourbaki expos\'e~577, June 1981.''',[F,'macdonald1981affine'],reconstruction=r'''The cocycle, zero modes and representation-space hypotheses are indispensable additions. The notebook outline is not an unrestricted construction from any list of Lorentzian vectors.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
add(660,'P001237','remark','Gelfand on the Monster, strings, and moonshine',
 r'''Gelfand connects the group of approximately \(10^{54}\) elements with strings, says ``the largest finite simple group'', and recalls work of Frenkel, Lepowsky and an unnamed third author.''',
 r'''The intended group is the Monster, the largest sporadic finite simple group; finite simple groups as a whole have unbounded orders. The third author is Arne Meurman. The mathematical connection is the construction of a graded Monster representation with normalized modular \(J\)-character, and the later proof of the moonshine conjectures by vertex-algebra and no-ghost methods.''',
 'E',r'''Frenkel--Lepowsky--Meurman announced their module in May 1984 \citep{frenkellepowskymeurman1984natural}, before this intervention. \citet[Theorem~1.1 and sections~5--6]{borcherds1992monstrous} subsequently proved the moonshine assertions. That later theorem is a development of the connection, not proof of a precise conjecture stated here.''', ['frenkellepowskymeurman1984natural',B],status=r'''As of November 2026, this is an identifiable pre-1985 mathematical connection with major later development. The notebook records no exact moonshine conjecture to classify separately.''',literal='false as written',match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
add(660,'P001238','construction','Null directions and transverse DDF operators',
 r'''For a null vector \(p\), the vectors \(v^1,\ldots,v^{d-2},p\) form a basis of \(p^\perp\). The residues \(U_n(v)=\int v(z)X(np,z)\,dz/z\) are asserted to be oscillators.''',
 r'''Let \(c\ne0\) be null in a real space of signature \((d-1,1)\). The quotient \(c^\perp/\mathbb Rc\) is positive definite of dimension \(d-2\). Choose orthonormal transverse representatives. Correctly normal-ordered DDF residues then satisfy \([A_m^i,A_n^j]=m\delta_{ij}\delta_{m,-n}(c,a_0)\); on a momentum tower with \((c,a_0)=1\), this is the standard Heisenberg relation. Sign changes in the exponential and mode labels must be made together.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[introduction, equations~(4)--(5), and section~4]{frenkel1985representations} gives the residues and their central factor. The original Del Giudice--Di Vecchia--Fubini construction dates to 1972, as identified in that bibliography.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''Nonzero \(c\), orthonormality in the quotient and normalization of its pairing with the momentum are absent from the notes and supplied here. Mere linear independence gives the Gram matrix in the commutator, not \(\delta_{ij}\).''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(660,'P001238','theorem','Completeness of transverse oscillators at the critical dimension',
 r'''After the Heisenberg assertion, the notes say that the operators generate the whole space.''',
 r'''At \(d=26\), fixed nonzero physical momentum and level, DDF transverse states provide representatives for every class in the physical quotient by null/spurious states. A chosen DDF vacuum generates its compatible momentum tower, not every momentum sector from one vector. At zero momentum the usual positive-definite completeness statement has an exceptional sector.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[section~4, Lemmas~4.2--4.3 and equations~(4.8)--(4.9)]{frenkel1985representations} supplies the critical-dimension transverse description.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The words ``whole space'' are restricted to the physical quotient and the momentum sectors on which the chosen DDF operators act.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(661,'P001238','theorem','Borel presentation of the cohomology of the full flag variety',
 r'''For complete flags in \(V^n\), the first description is \(H^*(X,\mathbb Q)=\mathbb Q[x_1,\ldots,x_n]/J\), where \(J\) is generated by symmetric polynomials with no constant term.''',
 r'''For \(X=\operatorname{Fl}(\mathbb C^n)\), let \(x_i\) be consistently signed first Chern classes of the tautological line quotients. Then \(H^*(X;\mathbb Q)\cong\mathbb Q[x_1,\ldots,x_n]/(e_1,\ldots,e_n)\), with \(\deg x_i=2\). The ideal equals that generated by all positive-degree symmetric polynomials.''',
 'E',r'''The Borel presentation is recalled by \citet[section~1]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}. The notebook explicitly presents the lecture as exposition of BGG and Lascoux--Sch\"utzenberger.''',[BG],reconstruction=r'''The field \(\mathbb C\), cohomological grading and signs of Chern generators are specified.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(661,'P001238-P001239','construction','Bruhat cells and the homology/cohomology bases',
 r'''The orbit of the permuted standard flag under transformations preserving the standard flag is \(C_w\), with complex dimension \(\ell(w)\). Cells are said to form the homology basis; dual classes have degree \(2\ell(w)\).''',
 r'''The Borel orbits \(C_w=BwB/B\) are affine spaces \(\mathbb C^{\ell(w)}\). Their closures \(\overline{C_w}\) have fundamental classes forming a basis of ordinary homology. The Kronecker-dual cohomology basis has degrees \(2\ell(w)\). With the standard opposite-Schubert convention, these are the Schubert classes represented by the polynomials below.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[section~2, in particular Theorem~2.11]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert} treats the cells and their closures. The passage from cells to fundamental classes of closures is essential.''',[BG],reconstruction=r'''The duality here is evaluation on homology classes, not an unqualified identification of a cell with its Poincar\'e-dual class of complementary degree.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(661,'P001239','construction','Schubert polynomial representatives',
 r'''BGG classes modulo \(J\) have been lifted to actual polynomials of degree \(\ell(w)\), with \(P_{\mathrm{id}}=1\) and \(P_{w_0}=x_1^{n-1}x_2^{n-2}\cdots x_{n-1}\).''',
 r'''The Lascoux--Sch\"utzenberger Schubert polynomial \(\mathfrak S_w\in\mathbb Z[x_1,x_2,\ldots]\) is homogeneous of ordinary polynomial degree \(\ell(w)\); for \(w\in S_n\), the longest-element polynomial is \(x^\delta=x_1^{n-1}x_2^{n-2}\cdots x_{n-1}\). Its class in the Borel quotient represents the corresponding Schubert class.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Definition~1.3]{lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert} defines these polynomials; the note appeared on 29 March 1982. Their 1983 longer account also predates this seminar \citep{lascouxschutzenberger1983symmetry}.''',[LS,'lascouxschutzenberger1983symmetry'],reconstruction=r'''The canonical choice involves stability and divided differences, not merely an arbitrary lifting of a residue class.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(661,'P001239','claim','Normalization of the top cohomology class',
 r'''The Vandermonde \(\prod_{i<j}(x_i-x_j)\) is called the same class modulo \(J\) as \(x^\delta\). An alternative is \(\rho^{m(n-1)/2}/(m(n-1)/2)!\).''',
 r'''Write \(N=n(n-1)/2\) and \(\rho=(n-1)x_1+(n-2)x_2+\cdots+x_{n-1}\). With compatible positive-root conventions, \[x^\delta\equiv\frac1{n!}\prod_{i<j}(x_i-x_j)\equiv\frac{\rho^N}{N!}\pmod J.\] The unnormalized Vandermonde represents \(n!\) times the top generator. Already for \(n=2\), \(x_1-x_2\equiv2x_1\).''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Theorem~3.15 and Corollary~3.16]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert} gives the normalization and the \(\rho\)-power formula. The scan confirms both the absent \(1/n!\) and the letter \(m\) in the exponent.''',[BG],literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(661,'P001240','construction','The sign convention for divided differences',
 r'''The numerator is \(f-s_if\), but the denominator is written \(x_{i+1}-x_i\).''',
 r'''The convention compatible with \(\mathfrak S_{w_0}=x^\delta\), \(\mathfrak S_{\mathrm{id}}=1\), and the usual positive Schubert polynomials is \[\partial_i f=\frac{f-s_if}{x_i-x_{i+1}}.\] This is again a polynomial. The opposite denominator changes the sign of each operator and hence contributes \((-1)^{\ell(w)}\) to a reduced product.''',
 'E',r'''Compare \citet[equations~(1.1)--(1.2)]{lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert} and \citet[Lemma~3.3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}. The smallest check is \(\partial_1x_1=1\); the notebook convention gives \(-1\).''',[LS,BG],reconstruction=r'''Opposite-sign divided differences are mathematically legitimate if every accompanying formula is adjusted. The recorded top and identity classes make the unadjusted collection inconsistent.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(661,'P001240','theorem','Independence of reduced expressions',
 r'''An operator \(A_w\) is obtained from a reduced product of adjacent transpositions and asserted independent of the decomposition. The last displayed transposition has a misplaced subscript.''',
 r'''The operators \(\partial_i\) satisfy \(\partial_i^2=0\), \(\partial_i\partial_j=\partial_j\partial_i\) for \(|i-j|>1\), and \(\partial_i\partial_{i+1}\partial_i=\partial_{i+1}\partial_i\partial_{i+1}\). Thus \(\partial_w\) is independent of a reduced expression \(w=s_{i_1}\cdots s_{i_{\ell(w)}}\). A nonreduced product of this length pattern vanishes when the corresponding word is not reduced.''',
 'E',r'''The exact reduced/nonreduced statement is \citet[Theorem~3.4]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert}.''',[BG],reconstruction=r'''The factors are adjacent simple reflections, not arbitrary inversions. The last factor is \((i_{\ell(w)},i_{\ell(w)}+1)\).''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(661,'P001239-P001240','theorem','Generating all Schubert polynomials from the longest element',
 r'''The displayed formula is \(P_w=A_{w_0w^{-1}}P_{w_0}\).''',
 r'''Under the usual left composition convention \(\partial_u=\partial_{i_1}\cdots\partial_{i_r}\) for \(u=s_{i_1}\cdots s_{i_r}\), one has \[\mathfrak S_w=\partial_{w^{-1}w_0}x^\delta.\] Equivalently, apply \(\partial_i\) whenever replacing the current permutation \(v\) by \(vs_i\) lowers its length. An opposite action convention reverses the product indexing.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Definition~1.3]{lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert} gives the construction; \citet[section~3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1973schubert} provides its cohomological precursor.''',[LS,BG],reconstruction=r'''The notebook does not specify left versus right operator action. Its \(w_0w^{-1}\) is therefore treated as a convention ambiguity rather than an isolated transcription error.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(661,'P001240','theorem','Symmetry at a non-descent',
 r'''\(P_w\) is symmetric in \(x_i,x_{i+1}\) if \((i,i+1)\) is not an inversion of \(w\).''',
 r'''For the usual Schubert polynomial, \(s_i\mathfrak S_w=\mathfrak S_w\) if and only if \(w(i)<w(i+1)\), equivalently \(\ell(ws_i)>\ell(w)\). Indeed \(\partial_i\mathfrak S_w=0\) in this case, whereas for a right descent it equals the nonzero polynomial \(\mathfrak S_{ws_i}\).''',
 'E',r'''The exact equivalence is stated immediately after \citet[Definition~1.3]{lascouxschutzenberger1982schubert}.''',[LS],reconstruction=r'''The notebook records the valid sufficient direction. ``Inversion'' is made precise as an adjacent right descent, not a value-based left descent.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(662,'P001241','construction','Normal ordering and the missing Sugawara half',
 r'''The lecture restarts with \(L_i=:\sum_{m+n=i}g_{\mu\nu}a_m^\mu a_n^\nu:\), followed by the standard Virasoro bracket. Normal ordering is said to make the expression meaningful and only add a constant.''',
 r'''The correctly normalized free-boson modes are \(L_i=\frac12\sum_{m+n=i}g_{\mu\nu}:a_m^\mu a_n^\nu:\). Moving annihilation modes to the right makes each mode locally finite on algebraic Fock vectors. Reordering a finite truncation changes it by a scalar, but the infinite unrenormalized scalar need not converge; it is not an ordinary finite constant that may simply be added.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[equation~(2.10) and the calculation following Theorem~3.3]{frenkel1985representations} fixes the normalization. The absent half is confirmed by the scan and contradicts the correct \(2L_m\) formula on P001233.''',[F],literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001241','question','Gelfand asks for normal ordering on other underlying spaces',
 r'''Gelfand asks what is needed for the operation \(:\ :\), and what happens for a lattice, torus or sphere.''',
 r'''The question asks which choices of geometry, modes, polarization and vacuum permit normal ordering and an analogue of the resulting centrally extended algebra. It does not say whether ``torus'' and ``sphere'' are world sheets, spatial manifolds or target spaces, nor which algebra should replace Virasoro.''',
 'F',r'''A precise nearby development is the higher-genus operator-field and normal-ordering formalism of Krichever--Novikov, begun in 1986 and described in \citet[section~2, Theorem~2.1]{krichevernovikov1989riemann}. That theorem establishes a Virasoro-type central extension for its own two-point Riemann-surface setup; it does not answer every interpretation of this intervention.''',[KN],reconstruction=r'''The two-point Riemann-surface formulation is a literature comparison, not a reconstructed exact question attributed to Gelfand.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, this remains an identifiable research direction but not a sufficiently specified original open problem. No C/D designation is warranted.''',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='read the result',confidence='medium',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
add(662,'P001241','construction','Coordinate and current Fourier series',
 r'''The notes list \(p^\mu(t)=\sum a_m^\mu e^{-imt}\), a coordinate series with coefficients \(a_m^\mu/m\), and \([x^\mu,a_0^\nu]=g^{\mu\nu}\), without the earlier factors of \(i\) or the linear zero-mode term.''',
 r'''In the Hermitian convention of the previous lecture, \([x^\mu,a_0^\nu]=ig^{\mu\nu}\) and \(Q^\mu(t)=x^\mu+a_0^\mu t+i\sum_{m\ne0}a_m^\mu e^{-imt}/m\), so \(\partial_tQ=P\). One can instead rescale generators in a complex algebraic convention, but then all factors of \(i\), zero modes and derivative identities must be adjusted together.''',
 'E',r'''The coordinate field is standard in \citet[introduction, equations~(1)--(3)]{frenkel1985representations}. The P001241 series is treated as abbreviated conventions, not as an independently valid derivative identity.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The formula restores the earlier P001233 convention rather than silently combining the two displays.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001241','remark','Gelfand identifies the classical vector fields and their central extension',
 r'''Gelfand writes \(L_n=e^{int}d/dt\) in the classical theory and compares its one-dimensional quantum extension with \([d/dx,x]=1\).''',
 r'''The complex vector fields \(D_n=i e^{int}d/dt\) satisfy \([D_m,D_n]=(m-n)D_{m+n}\). Their standard nontrivial one-dimensional central extension has cocycle \((m^3-m)\delta_{m,-n}/12\), giving Virasoro. The displayed unscaled fields satisfy the same relation with an extra factor of \(i\). The Heisenberg comparison is structural, not an identification of the two cocycles.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[equations~(2.8)--(2.9)]{frenkel1985representations} states the vector-field/central-extension relation before this talk.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''A normalization of the Fourier vector fields is supplied to match the notebook bracket.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
add(662,'P001241','claim','When the Virasoro Fock module depends only on momentum norm',
 r'''The notes assert \(\mathcal H_p\cong\mathcal H_{p'}\) whenever \((p,p)=(p',p')\), and label the module only by this scalar.''',
 r'''An isometry of the target form carrying \(p\) to \(p'\) induces a Virasoro-module isomorphism. In a real Lorentzian space, equal nonzero norms give such an isometry, as do two nonzero null vectors. Zero and a nonzero null vector are an exception despite equal norm: on level one, \(L_1(a_{-1}(v)|p\rangle)=(v,p)|p\rangle\), a rank-zero map for \(p=0\) and rank-one map for \(p\ne0\). Hence these graded Virasoro modules are not isomorphic.''',
 'E',r'''The countercheck is immediate from the standard relations in \citet[equations~(2.2), (2.10), (2.14)]{frenkel1985representations}. It is a correction of an expository claim, not a new unresolved representation problem.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The isometry-orbit argument gives a sufficient statement. The level-one calculation isolates a literal counterexample in the Lorentzian setting actually used by these lectures.''',status=r'''As of November 2026, the norm-only assertion is false without excluding the zero/nonzero-null distinction.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001241-P001242','construction','Summing momentum sectors before imposing the constraints',
 r'''The module \(\bigoplus_{(p,p)\in\mathbb R}\mathcal H_{(p,p)}\) is used to construct physical states, with the comment that the construction depends only on the Virasoro action.''',
 r'''One may form an algebraic direct sum of chosen Virasoro modules and impose \(P^1\) and its spurious quotient componentwise. For the subsequent vertex action, however, the summands must retain actual momenta in a set closed under the required shifts; one representative for each numerical norm does not specify those maps. Positivity also uses the invariant form, not the abstract Virasoro action alone.''',
 'E',r'''The momentum-labelled lattice sum \(S(\mathfrak h^-)\otimes\mathbb C[\Gamma]\) is explicit in \citet[section~2.1, equation~(2.2)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The statement separates a legitimate abstract module sum from the extra momentum and form data required later.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
add(662,'P001242','theorem','The repeated no-ghost statement uses the quotient, not all constrained states',
 r'''The theorem says that twenty-four commuting oscillators generate \(\widetilde{\mathcal H}\) from a vacuum, and that its inherited form is positive definite, only when \(d=26\).''',
 r'''In the critical Lorentzian bosonic theory at nonzero momentum, constrained physical states have a positive semidefinite form; the quotient by its radical is positive definite and is represented by transverse DDF states. Spurious null vectors remain before quotienting. A single chosen DDF vacuum generates a normalized momentum tower, not the unrestricted direct sum of all momenta.''',
 'E',r'''The exact qualifications are given in \citet[Theorem~4.1 and section~4]{frenkel1985representations}; compare \citet[Theorem~5.1]{borcherds1992monstrous}. The positive-semidefinite assertion also has subcritical variants, so ``only 26'' should refer to the precise twenty-four-oscillator critical completeness statement.''',[F,B],reconstruction=r'''The inserted tilde in the manuscript is preserved in notes but corrected in the mathematical statement. Zero momentum is again excluded.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001242','construction','The corrected oscillator vertex formula still omits zero-mode powers',
 r'''Equation (9) now includes \(1/n\) in both exponentials and defines \(D_p|k\rangle=|k+p\rangle\).''',
 r'''This is a valid oscillator-stripped momentum-shifting series. To obtain the plane primary field one must also include \(z^{(p,a_0)}\); the cylinder-normalized field includes \(z^{(p,p)/2}\) as well. Integral pairings are needed for unshifted integral Laurent modes on a lattice sum.''',
 'E',r'''The complete expression and mode convention are \citet[equation~(2.3)]{frenkel1985representations}. This passage repairs the denominator missing on P001237, but does not itself restore the zero modes.''',[F],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(662,'P001242','claim','The Laurent-mode sign in the Heisenberg commutator',
 r'''The expansion is \(X(p,z)=\sum_iX_i(p)z^i\), while equation (12) asserts \([a_n^\mu,X_i(p)]=p^\mu X_{i+n}(p)\).''',
 r'''The field relation is \([a_n^\mu,X(p,z)]=p^\mu z^nX(p,z)\). With \(X=\sum_iX_i z^i\), coefficient comparison gives \([a_n^\mu,X_i]=p^\mu X_{i-n}\). The recorded plus sign is correct only for the alternative expansion \(X=\sum_iX_i z^{-i}\).''',
 'E',r'''The negative-exponent convention occurs in \citet[equations~(2.3)--(2.4)]{frenkel1985representations}. Both conflicting signs in the notebook are clear in the scan.''',[F],literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001242','definition','Integral, even, and unimodular lattices',
 r'''The lattice \(Q\subset\mathbb R^d\) is integral and unimodular when its Gram entries are integers and their determinant is one. Evenness is \((\gamma,\gamma)\in2\mathbb Z\).''',
 r'''A full-rank lattice in a nondegenerate real quadratic space is integral if \((Q,Q)\subset\mathbb Z\), even if every norm is even, and unimodular if \(Q=Q^*\), equivalently the Gram determinant has absolute value one. In the positive-definite case used for the finite root system below, this determinant is \(+1\).''',
 'E',r'''These standard definitions are recalled in \citet[section~1]{brouwerleech}. They precede the seminar; the reference is an undated modern exposition, not a priority claim.''',['brouwerleech'],reconstruction=r'''The rank and signature assumptions are stated. Indefinite unimodular Gram determinants can be \(-1\).''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(662,'P001243','definition','Norm-two roots of an integral positive-definite lattice',
 r'''A root is a lattice vector of squared length two; later \(\Delta\) denotes the set of all such vectors and positivity is imposed.''',
 r'''For a positive-definite integral lattice \(Q\), \(\Delta=\{\alpha\in Q:(\alpha,\alpha)=2\}\) is finite. The reflections \(s_\alpha(v)=v-(v,\alpha)\alpha\) preserve \(Q\) and \(\Delta\). Thus \(\Delta\) is a reduced simply laced crystallographic root system in its span, possibly empty or reducible; its components have types \(A,D,E\).''',
 'E',r'''The finite simply laced root-system construction is the setting of \citet[section~1.1, equation~(1.3)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''Positivity is essential to finiteness. Roots need not span the entire lattice space.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(662,'P001243','theorem','The rank restriction for even unimodular positive lattices',
 r'''Immediately after defining roots, the notes say ``such'' objects exist only in dimensions divisible by eight.''',
 r'''A positive-definite even unimodular lattice has rank divisible by eight. Conversely, for every positive multiple \(8r\), the orthogonal sum \(E_8^{\oplus r}\) provides one. This statement concerns lattices with all three hypotheses, not norm-two roots; for example the rank-one root lattice \(A_1\) already has roots.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Theorem~1.2]{brouwerleech} states the divisibility result and its theta-series context. This is a classical theorem, not a post-1985 development.''',['brouwerleech'],reconstruction=r'''The antecedent of ``such'' is resolved using the preceding integral/even/unimodular assumptions. In indefinite signature the appropriate restriction is on the signature difference.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(662,'P001243','construction','The eight-dimensional lattice exercise',
 r'''The exercise is to construct such a lattice in \(\mathbb R^8\).''',
 r'''Let \(D_8=\{x\in\mathbb Z^8:\sum_i x_i\equiv0\pmod2\}\) and \(h=(1/2,\ldots,1/2)\). Then \(E_8=D_8\cup(D_8+h)\) is a lattice: \(2h\in D_8\). It is integral and even since \((h,h)=2\) and \((x,h)=\frac12\sum x_i\in\mathbb Z\) on \(D_8\). Its covolume is \(\operatorname{covol}(D_8)/2=1\), hence it is unimodular.''',
 'E',r'''This explicit standard construction solves the notebook exercise; \citet[section~1]{brouwerleech} supplies the surrounding even-unimodular lattice context. No novelty is claimed for the construction.''',['brouwerleech'],reconstruction=r'''The coordinates and the elementary verification are supplied by the companion.''',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001243','claim','ADE root lattices are even',
 r'''An aside says that root lattices of the \(A,D,E\) series satisfy condition (14), namely evenness.''',
 r'''With every root normalized to squared length two, an integral linear combination of simple roots has even norm, because diagonal terms are even and cross terms occur twice. Thus simply laced root lattices are even. They need not be unimodular; evenness, not condition (13), is the recorded assertion.''',
 'E',r'''The normalization and root-lattice setting are in \citet[section~1.1]{frenkel1985representations}. The elementary expansion proves the exact notebook assertion.''',[F],literal='correct',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001243','construction','The sign cocycle forces evenness',
 r'''The lattice is now said not necessarily to be even, yet \(\varepsilon(\alpha,\beta)\varepsilon(\beta,\alpha)=(-1)^{(\alpha,\beta)}\) is required for every pair, with \(\varepsilon(\alpha,\alpha)=-1\) on roots.''',
 r'''For an even integral free abelian lattice, choose a bimultiplicative normalized cocycle \(\varepsilon:Q\times Q\to\{\pm1\}\) with commutator \((-1)^{(\alpha,\beta)}\) and diagonal \(\varepsilon(\alpha,\alpha)=(-1)^{(\alpha,\alpha)/2}\). Such a choice is made on an ordered lattice basis and extended multiplicatively. The displayed commutator identity itself forces evenness on setting \(\alpha=\beta\), since the left side equals one. An arbitrary function with the two displayed identities need not satisfy the cocycle identity.''',
 'E',r'''The word ``bilinear'' and the existence argument on a basis are explicit in \citet[equation~(1.2) and following paragraph]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''Evenness and bimultiplicativity are restored. Odd-lattice superalgebra conventions require a different commutator sign and are not silently substituted.''',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001243','construction','A finite Lie algebra from the lattice roots',
 r'''The space \(\mathfrak g=\mathfrak h\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in\Delta}\mathbb Cx_\alpha\) is given root brackets using \(\varepsilon\), including \([x_\alpha,x_{-\alpha}]=-\alpha\), and called an important construction of simple algebras.''',
 r'''For a positive-definite even integral lattice and the cocycle just specified, set \([h,h']=0\), \([h,x_\alpha]=(h,\alpha)x_\alpha\), use \(\varepsilon(\alpha,\beta)x_{\alpha+\beta}\) when \(\alpha+\beta\in\Delta\), zero when the sum is neither a root nor zero, and \([x_\alpha,x_{-\alpha}]=-\alpha\). This gives a reductive complex Lie algebra. It is semisimple when roots span \(\mathfrak h\), and simple when they also form an irreducible root system. The orthogonal complement of their span is central.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[equation~(1.3)]{frenkel1985representations} gives the same sign convention for the irreducible ADE case.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The Cartan bracket and the spanning/irreducibility hypotheses are supplied. For a rootless lattice the displayed algebra is abelian, not simple.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
add(662,'P001243-P001244','construction','Twisted lattice vertices and the affine basic representation',
 r'''The factor \(\varepsilon_\gamma|p\rangle=\varepsilon(\gamma,p)|p\rangle\) modifies the vertex field, and its modes together with Heisenberg modes are said to give a projective current-algebra representation.''',
 r'''For an ADE root lattice \(Q\), the space \(S(\bigoplus_{n>0}(Q\otimes\mathbb C)t^{-n})\otimes\mathbb C[Q]\), with the corrected zero-mode factors and lattice cocycle, is the level-one basic module of the untwisted affine algebra of the corresponding finite simple Lie algebra. More general momentum lattices between the root and weight lattices decompose into level-one modules by root-lattice cosets; an arbitrary even unimodular lattice is not automatically a single ADE basic module.''',
 'E',r'''The original construction is \citet{frenkelkac1980basic}, published in 1980. The exact representation statement is also \citet[Theorem~2.2]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[FK,F],reconstruction=r'''The finite ADE algebra, root lattice, level and completion conventions are specified. The result is linear for the central extension and projective for the unextended loop algebra.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(662,'P001244','remark','The special rank-twenty-four lattice and sporadic groups',
 r'''A special lattice in \(\mathbb R^{24}\) is said to occur here and in finite simple groups; the number of sporadic simple groups is twenty-six.''',
 r'''The intended lattice is the Leech lattice: up to isometry it is the unique positive-definite even unimodular rank-twenty-four lattice with no norm-two vectors. Its symmetry groups and the associated vertex constructions connect to sporadic groups and moonshine. Under the standard convention excluding the Tits group, there are twenty-six sporadic simple groups.''',
 'E',r'''\citet[Theorem~1.1]{brouwerleech} states the precise lattice characterization. The maintained Atlas lists the twenty-six groups \citep{wilsonparkerbray2003sporadic}; the pre-seminar Monster-module link is identified by \citet{frenkellepowskymeurman1984natural}.''',['brouwerleech','wilsonparkerbray2003sporadic','frenkellepowskymeurman1984natural'],reconstruction=r'''The name Leech is inferred from the rank and group-theoretic context. Its absence of roots means that the preceding norm-two-current construction alone yields no nonabelian root currents for this lattice.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',confidence='high')
add(662,'P001244','theorem','The current-algebra brackets and the omitted opposite-root case',
 r'''The brackets vanish for \((\gamma_1,\gamma_2)>0\), give a signed \(\gamma_1+\gamma_2\) mode for inner product \(-1\), and other relations are left unwritten.''',
 r'''With consistent negative-exponent mode labels, lattice root currents satisfy the affine relations \[[x\otimes t^m,y\otimes t^n]=[x,y]\otimes t^{m+n}+m\delta_{m,-n}\kappa(x,y)I.\] For roots, nonnegative inner product gives commuting modes, inner product \(-1\) gives the signed sum-root mode, and opposite roots supply the Cartan mode and the central term. In the convention \([x_\alpha,x_{-\alpha}]=-\alpha\), one takes \(\kappa(x_\alpha,x_{-\alpha})=-1\).''',
 'E',r'''All three cases occur in \citet[equations~(1.4), (2.4), Theorem~2.2]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The notebook gives valid partial cases but does not display the central term responsible for projectivity. Positive-exponent mode labels reverse indices and must be handled consistently.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
add(662,'P001244','construction','The final DDF residue recipe',
 r'''Equations (26)--(30) use a null \(c\), a current with polarization \(\alpha\perp c\), and twenty-four such polarizations forming with \(c\) a basis of \(c^\perp\).''',
 r'''Take \(c\ne0\) null in signature \((25,1)\), choose an orthonormal basis of \(c^\perp/\mathbb Rc\), and use the normal-ordered residues of the corresponding currents times \(Y_{nc}\). On a sector with the appropriate normalized value of \((c,a_0)\), these are twenty-four Heisenberg oscillators commuting with Virasoro; their classes generate the nonzero-momentum physical quotient as in record~660.''',
 'E',r'''This repeats the construction in \citet[equations~(4)--(5) and (4.8)--(4.9)]{frenkel1985representations}.''',[F],reconstruction=r'''The omitted momentum normalization, normal ordering and orthonormal quotient basis are restored. The displayed arbitrary basis alone does not imply standard delta-normalized brackets.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
talks=[
 dict(record=658,date='1985-11-11',speaker='A. M. Vershik',pages='P001229-P001231',title='Configuration spaces, polytope universality and lattices',kind='talk',summary=r'''The talk moves from Grassmannian configuration strata and isotopy of determinant-sign classes to realization spaces of polytopes. Mn\"ev universality was already circulating and published; the statement about simplicial four-polytopes retains an informal hedge and is not established by either general four-dimensional universality or variable-dimensional neighborly universality. The final part concerns geometric lattices, the Fano obstruction, lattice representations and the Pudl\'ak--T\r{u}ma theorem. Gelfand adds a historical intervention. The manuscript begins on P001229 and ends on the upper part of P001231; the supplied catalogue range overlaps adjacent talks.'''),
 dict(record=659,date='1985-11-11',speaker='B. L. Feigin',pages='P001231-P001235',title='Dual amplitudes, free bosons and vertex operators',kind='talk',summary=r'''The first string lecture introduces elastic-scattering invariants and the Veneziano amplitude, then reconstructs amplitudes through oscillator Fock spaces, Virasoro modes, normal-ordered exponential vertices and contraction identities. The notebook asserts uniqueness under conditions that do not even fix an overall normalization. Its five-point evaluation remark lacks the exponents and a specified meaning of evaluation, so no open problem is manufactured. These are mainly established constructions; zero modes, metric signs and plane versus cylinder conventions account for several essential corrections. The Feigin heading occurs midway down P001231.'''),
 dict(record=660,date='1985-11-18',speaker='B. L. Feigin',pages='P001236-P001238',title='Physical quotients, the no-ghost theorem and lattice vertices',kind='talk',summary=r'''Feigin defines the constrained physical space and spurious quotient, states the critical no-ghost result, and connects screening residues with Kac--Moody algebras and transverse DDF oscillators. Gelfand identifies the Monster/string connection. The strongest needed qualifications are the nonzero-momentum exception, the quotient by null states, the distinction between ghosts and tachyons, and the momentum normalization in DDF brackets. The last part on P001238 ends immediately before Zelevinsky begins; it does not include that page in its entirety.'''),
 dict(record=661,date='1985-11-18',speaker='A. V. Zelevinsky',pages='P001238-P001240',title='Schubert cells, divided differences and Schubert polynomials',kind='talk',summary=r'''This is explicitly an exposition of Lascoux--Sch\"utzenberger, following the 1973 Bernstein--Gelfand--Gelfand paper. It compares the Borel presentation with the Schubert basis and constructs polynomial representatives by divided differences. The missing top-class factor \(1/n!\) and the divided-difference sign are genuine mathematical inconsistencies in the manuscript. Earlier related occurrences include 1971-239.1--2 (Schubert homology), 1979-483.1 (reduced words), 1979-484.3 (Schubert closures), and 1982-575.1 (Grassmannian cohomology); no earlier problem identifier matches a new open question here.'''),
 dict(record=662,date='1985-11-25',speaker='B. L. Feigin',pages='P001241-P001244',title='Normal ordering, even lattices and affine current representations',kind='talk',summary=r'''The continuation revisits Virasoro and no-ghost, then constructs root Lie algebras and affine current representations from even lattices and sign cocycles, concluding with the DDF recipe. Gelfand asks how normal ordering depends on the underlying geometry. Krichever--Novikov supplies a nearby later construction, but the intervention does not specify a unique theorem. The module assertion depending only on momentum norm fails at zero versus nonzero null momentum; the sign cocycle itself forces the evenness temporarily waived in the notes. The Leech-lattice aside must be kept distinct from the norm-two-root construction because that lattice is rootless. The next dated talk starts on P001245 and is outside this record.''')
]
problems=[
 dict(id='GP-1985-40',title='Topology of real configuration realization spaces',statement=r'''For a prescribed finite rank configuration or oriented-matroid type, determine the topology of its normalized real realization space; in particular, determine what homotopy types can occur. Incidence types and uniform chirotopes are distinguished. The exact modern universality theorem concerns stable equivalence to primary basic semialgebraic sets, with the uniform case restricted to open sets.''',occurrences=['1985-658.1','1985-658.6','1985-658.16'],status=r'''As of November 2026, general rank-three universality is established and was already announced before the talk by \citet[Theorem~3]{mnev1985varieties}. The modern formulation is \citet[Theorem~1]{adiprasitopadrol2017universality}. No claim of literal homeomorphism to every arbitrary algebraic variety is intended.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=[M,AP]),
 dict(id='GP-1985-41',title='Isotopy within a fixed determinant-sign class',statement=r'''For a uniform realizable rank-three oriented matroid on \(n\) labelled elements, is its normalized real realization space path connected? Equivalently, can matrices with the same nonzero maximal-minor signs be joined through the same sign class after fixing an oriented basis?''',occurrences=['1985-658.3','1985-658.4','1985-658.5'],status=r'''As of November 2026, the general answer is negative by universality, already available before this talk. The small-size assertion \(n\leq7\) is supported by a stronger known bound cited in \citet[introduction]{tsukamoto2013disconnected}. The exact uniform nineteen-element example attributed to Mn\"ev in the notebook remains an unresolved source identification; this does not make the general mathematical question open.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=[M,AP,TS]),
 dict(id='GP-1985-42',title='Universality in dimension four under simpliciality',statement=r'''The notebook informally suggests ``more or less any topology'' for fixed-type simplicial four-polytope realization spaces. An editorial homotopy sharpening asks whether every nonempty finite simplicial-complex homotopy type occurs. The stronger precise modern conjecture asks whether every nonempty open primary basic semialgebraic set defined over \(\mathbb Z\) is stably equivalent to the homogenized realization space, modulo \(\mathrm{GL}_5(\mathbb R)\), of a neighborly (hence simplicial) four-polytope.''',occurrences=['1985-658.10'],status=r'''As of November 2026, no resolution of either fixed-four-dimensional universality formulation was found in the bounded search. General four-polytopes are universal by \citet[Main Theorem]{richtergebertziegler1995realization}; simplicial neighborly universality in \citet[Theorem~2]{adiprasitopadrol2017universality} allows growing dimension, and their Conjecture~4 retains dimension four and additionally requires neighborliness. The latest opened explicit open-status evidence is the 2024 compendium CG68--CG69; this is not an exhaustive proof of absence of a later result. The chapter establishes only contractibility for normalized history-compatible spaces of prescribed facet-stackings.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=[RG,AP,'schaefercardinalmiltzow2024existential'])
]

own_checks={
'1985-658.2':r'''\ours\ The factorization through \((\ker A)^\perp\) is the elementary proof supplied here; no universality assertion is being proved by it.''',
'1985-659.1':r'''\ours\ Bilinear expansion, using \(p_2+q_2=p_1+q_1\) and the four mass-shell equations, verifies \(s+t+u=4m^2\).''',
'1985-659.3':r'''\ours\ If \(A\) satisfies the listed homogeneous conditions, so does \(cA\) for every nonzero constant \(c\); this supplies the stated countercheck of uniqueness without normalization.''',
'1985-659.6':r'''\ours\ Expanding \((1-xy)^{-e}\) and integrating each power gives the displayed beta factors and the \({}_3F_2\) series. \oursComputed\ The terminating specialization \(a=b=c=d=1,e=-2\) gives \(11/18\) independently by polynomial integration and by the hypergeometric expression; code and output are supplied. This checks an editorial model, not missing notebook exponents.''',
'1985-659.8':r'''\ours\ On \(0<|u|<2\pi\), the Fourier identity \(\sum_{n\ne0}e^{-inu}/n=-i(\pi\operatorname{sgn}u-u)\), together with the zero-mode commutator \(-iu\), yields the stated odd kernel. Differentiating the distributional series gives the two current commutators.''',
'1985-659.12':r'''\ours\ The spectral formula follows by applying the displayed operator integral to an \(L_0\)-eigenvector and then recognizing Euler's beta integral.''',
'1985-659.14':r'''\ours\ The oscillator commutator of the two exponential exponents is the scalar \(-(k_1,k_2)\sum_{n>0}z^n/n\); exponentiation and \(-\log(1-z)=\sum_{n>0}z^n/n\) give the generating series.''',
'1985-660.2':r'''\ours\ At a fixed finite-dimensional energy level, adjointness gives \(P^1=S^\perp\); nondegeneracy of the ambient form gives \((P^1)^\perp=S\). Thus its radical is \(P^1\cap S\), verifying the corrected quotient algebraically.''',
'1985-660.3':r'''\ours\ For a weight-\(h\) vector, the bracket gives \(L_0L_{-1}v'=(h+1)L_{-1}v'\). A nonzero weight-one image forces \(h=0\).''',
'1985-660.5':r'''\ours\ The zero-momentum exception can be checked directly: every level-one vector \(a_{-1}(v)|0\rangle\) obeys all positive-mode constraints and inherits the target-space form, which is indefinite. This is a check of a known exception, not a proof of no-ghost.''',
'1985-660.6':r'''\ours\ In a single mode, represent \(a_{-n}\) by multiplication by \(u\) and \(a_n\) by \(n\partial_u\). The creation exponential then has the required commutator precisely with coefficient \(1/n\). \oursComputed\ This differential-operator identity was checked for frequencies one through six on \(u^3+u+1\); omitting the denominator multiplies the result by \(n\).''',
'1985-660.7':r'''\ours\ Acting from right to left, the momentum immediately before \(Y_{p_i}\) is \(k+\sum_{j>i}p_j\), which gives the displayed zero-mode monomial. This verifies that the stripped contraction and full matrix element differ.''',
'1985-660.8':r'''\ours\ The residue of the derivative of any integral Laurent series is zero, proving the displayed commutation assertion from the primary-field transformation law.''',
'1985-660.9':r'''\ours\ The Gram matrix is the pullback of the ambient form under the column map \(\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^{26}\); a positive or negative definite subspace maps injectively into one of the corresponding sign in the target. This proves the stated necessary inertia bounds.''',
'1985-661.4':r'''\ours\ The \(n=2\) identity \(x_2\equiv-x_1\pmod J\) already detects the missing factor. \oursComputed\ Exact Gr\"obner reductions for \(n=2,3,4\) separately checked the Vandermonde and \(\rho\)-power normalizations. The general identities are the cited BGG results.''',
'1985-661.5':r'''\ours\ The countercheck \(\partial_1x_1=1\) determines the compatible denominator sign. \oursComputed\ Exact polynomial calculations also checked square-zero and the braid identity on a nontrivial input and the longest-word top-to-one recurrence for \(n=2,3,4\). These finite audits do not replace the cited general theorem.''',
'1985-662.1':r'''\ours\ At zero momentum, \(L_{-2}|0\rangle=\frac12\sum_{\mu,\nu}g_{\mu\nu}a_{-1}^\mu a_{-1}^\nu|0\rangle\); the corresponding annihilation operator gives \(L_2L_{-2}|0\rangle=(d/2)|0\rangle\). Omitting both halves gives \(2d\), inconsistent with the listed central term. The same central coefficient is obtained with positive-sign species because the metric contraction is \(g_{\mu\nu}g^{\mu\nu}=d\) in either signature. \oursComputed\ Exact oscillator-polynomial computations give these values for \(d=1,2,26\).''',
'1985-662.5':r'''\ours\ The level-one rank calculation supplies a literal counterexample to the norm-only assertion. \oursComputed\ A two-dimensional Lorentzian block with momenta \(0\) and \((1,1)\) has equal norm zero but the two ranks are zero and one; the block embeds in the twenty-six-dimensional target.''',
'1985-662.9':r'''\ours\ The coefficient of \(z^i\) in \(z^n\sum_jX_jz^j\) is \(X_{i-n}\). \oursComputed\ Twenty-five exact Laurent-coefficient comparisons confirm this indexing; the general correction is the coefficient identity itself.''',
'1985-662.13':r'''\ours\ The parity, integrality and index-two covolume verification constitute the supplied proof of the standard \(E_8\) exercise.''',
'1985-662.14':r'''\ours\ For \(v=\sum n_i\alpha_i\), the norm is \(2\sum_i n_i^2+2\sum_{i<j}n_in_j(\alpha_i,\alpha_j)\), an even integer.''',
'1985-662.15':r'''\ours\ Substitution \(\beta=\alpha\) gives \(1=(-1)^{(\alpha,\alpha)}\), directly contradicting the allowance of odd norms.''',
'1985-662.16':r'''\ours\ If \(\Delta=\varnothing\), the displayed vector space is just the abelian Cartan space, which checks why simplicity cannot be claimed under the notebook assumptions alone.'''
}
for item in items:
    if item['id'] in own_checks:
        item['reconstruction']=(item['reconstruction']+' '+own_checks[item['id']]).strip()

# Normalize typographical commands without changing the three evidentiary layers.
for obj in items+talks+problems:
    for key,value in list(obj.items()):
        if isinstance(value,str):
            obj[key]=value.replace(r'\textquotesingle s',"'s").replace(r'\textquotesingle',"'")
for name,data in [('items',items),('talks',talks),('problems',problems)]:
    (out/f'{name}.json').write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Saved',len(talks),'talks,',len(items),'items,',len(problems),'problems')
from collections import Counter
print('Per record',dict(Counter(x['record'] for x in items)))
print('Classes',dict(Counter(x['class'] for x in items)))
